const title = "Whirlpool Ice Maker Not Working? Causes & Fix — Same Day";
const description = "Whirlpool refrigerator not making ice? Start with the filter, supply, and freezer temp, then know which of the two Whirlpool ice-maker types you have — modular or optical — and how to reset it. Safe checks inside.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my Whirlpool refrigerator not making ice?",
    a: "Work the water path first. The common operator-fixable causes are a clogged water filter (replace every six months), a supply valve that's partly closed or a kinked line, a frozen fill tube, a freezer that's too warm to cycle (it needs to be around 0°F), and the ice maker being switched off or its feeler arm stuck up. If those are clear, the fault is usually the water inlet valve or the ice maker itself. Whirlpool uses two ice-maker designs — a modular crescent-mold type and an in-door optical (infrared) type — and knowing which you have points the diagnosis, which we cover below."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I reset a Whirlpool ice maker?",
    a: "First make sure the ice maker is on and the feeler (shut-off) arm is down. Then, per Whirlpool's own guidance: if your ice maker has a reset button, press and hold it for a few seconds; if it doesn't, power-cycle the refrigerator by unplugging it for about a minute. On the modular crescent-mold type you can also cycle the feeler arm — lift it to the off position, wait, then lower it — to force a harvest. On the in-door optical type, running the fridge's ice-maker diagnostic and then unplugging for about 10 seconds resets the optics. After any reset, give it several hours to make the first batch."
  },
  {
    q: "What's the difference between Whirlpool's two ice makers?",
    a: "The modular crescent-mold ice maker sits in the freezer, chills a metal mold until a thermostat closes, then heats and ejects crescent-shaped cubes while a wire feeler arm shuts it off when the bin is full. The in-door optical ice maker instead uses an infrared emitter and receiver to sense the ice level. They fail differently: the modular type's usual faults are the mold heater, the thermostat, or the motor; the optical type's usual fault is the emitter/receiver board misreading the bin. Our techs identify which you have and test the right parts rather than guessing."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I fix a Whirlpool ice maker that won't work myself?",
    a: "Several parts of it, yes. Replace the water filter if it's overdue, confirm the supply valve is open and the line isn't kinked, make sure the ice maker is on with the arm down, verify the freezer is cold enough, and thaw a frozen fill tube gently. A reset (button or power-cycle) clears many transient faults. What you shouldn't do is replace the water inlet valve or open the ice maker module — those are electrically live, plumbed-in tech jobs. If it still won't make ice after the water path is confirmed, that's the handoff point."
  },
  {
    q: "Is it worth repairing a Whirlpool ice maker?",
    a: "Usually yes. The no-ice fixes — a filter, a fill-tube thaw, a water inlet valve, or the ice maker assembly — are affordable against a refrigerator you're keeping. Our techs verify the water path first (a clogged filter starves the maker and mimics a dead part), then test the valve and the maker before quoting. In LA's hard water, mineral scale on the inlet-valve screen and fill tube is a common flow restriction that's often the whole fix. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair."
  }
];

